<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ .Title }}</title>
<style>
  /* Colours come from docs/assets/css/kartoza-palette.json, the same source the
     documentation CSS and the generated diagrams read, delivered here as
     .Brand.* fields.

     The brand rules this sheet is held to are the ones written at the top of
     docs/assets/css/custom.css, because a report that contradicts the
     documentation is not branded, it is merely coloured:

       · Flat. No shadows, bevels, glows or gradients on any element.
       · Charcoal carries body text and the masthead. Blue is the primary
         accent, amber the secondary.
       · Blue and amber are never body text — they fail WCAG AA on white.
         Amber 700 measures 2.85:1 on white, so every amber word in this report
         is amber 900 instead (5.52:1).
       · Nunito throughout, JetBrains Mono for figures.

     Every foreground/background pair below was measured against WCAG 2.2 AA;
     the ratio is recorded wherever the pair is not obviously safe.

     The typeface itself is embedded, in the second <style> block at the end of
     this file's <head>. See the comment there for why. */
  :root {
    --blue:{{ .Brand.Blue500 }}; --blue-100:{{ .Brand.Blue100 }}; --blue-300:{{ .Brand.Blue300 }};
    --blue-900:{{ .Brand.Blue900 }};
    --amber:{{ .Brand.Amber500 }}; --amber-100:{{ .Brand.Amber100 }}; --amber-300:{{ .Brand.Amber300 }};
    --amber-900:{{ .Brand.Amber900 }};
    --ink:{{ .Brand.InkDefault }}; --muted:{{ .Brand.InkMuted }}; --rule:{{ .Brand.InkRule }};
    --cloud:{{ .Brand.SurfaceCloud }}; --white:{{ .Brand.SurfaceWhite }};
    --success:{{ .Brand.Success }};
    --error:{{ .Brand.Error }}; --error-tint:{{ .Brand.ErrorTint }};

    /* The type stacks are written out rather than interpolated from
       .Brand.FontSans / .Brand.FontMono.

       html/template refuses to substitute those two values in a CSS context:
       they contain quotes and commas, which its sanitiser cannot prove safe, so
       it replaced each whole declaration with the string ZgotmplZ. Every report
       ever produced by this template has therefore been set in the browser's
       default face, and every "monospace" figure column in a proportional one.
       Grep a generated report for ZgotmplZ to see it.

       Hard-coding is the lesser evil until Brand.FontSans and Brand.FontMono
       are typed template.CSS in brand.go, at which point these two lines go
       back to interpolating them.

       Both stacks lead with an embedded face, and that order is not a
       preference — it is the only order that renders.

       Measured on a font-less container: with the embedded face placed *last*,
       behind Nunito and the usual Helvetica/Arial insurance, headless Chromium
       printed a blank document. It resolves the first family, finds nothing,
       and its fallback path gives up before it reaches the @font-face rule.
       Move the same face to the front of the same stack and every glyph
       appears. Reproduce with /tmp probes or trust the comment, but do not
       "tidy" the palette's families back to the front.

       "Report Sans" and "Report Mono" are aliases, not typefaces. They mean
       "whichever face this report embeds", so the day Nunito is vendored as
       woff2 only the payload at the foot of this <head> changes and nothing
       here moves. The palette families still follow them, as insurance for the
       case where the data URI itself fails to decode. */
    --sans: "Report Sans", {{ .Brand.FontSans }};
    --mono: "Report Mono", {{ .Brand.FontMono }};

    /* Figures line up because they are asked to, not because the face happens
       to be monospaced. Applied wherever a number can change width. */
    --figures: tabular-nums lining-nums;
  }

  /* 15mm at the head, 16mm elsewhere. No bleed: the masthead sits inside the
     page margin, because most office printers cannot bleed and a masthead
     clipped at the paper edge reads as a fault rather than a choice. */
  @page { size: A4; margin: 15mm 16mm 16mm; }

  * { box-sizing: border-box; }

  body {
    margin: 0; padding: 0;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    /* ss01/ss02 are Nunito's alternate letterforms; the documentation sets the
       same pair, and matching them is most of why the two read as one family.
       They are inert in the fallback faces. */
    font-feature-settings: "ss01" 1, "ss02" 1;
    color: var(--ink); background: var(--cloud);
    font-size: 10.5pt; line-height: 1.5;
    -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact; print-color-adjust: exact;
  }

  /* On screen the report is a sheet of paper on a desk. Flat, per the brand
     rules — the edge is a hairline, not a shadow. */
  .sheet {
    max-width: 210mm; margin: 0 auto; padding: 15mm 16mm 0;
    background: var(--white); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  /* ---- masthead ------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Charcoal, like the documentation's header bar, closed with the amber
     keyline the documentation puts under every h2. The previous blue gradient
     broke two brand rules at once: it was a gradient, and its pale end ran
     white type onto blue 500 at 2.83:1, well under AA. */
  .masthead {
    background: var(--ink); color: var(--white);
    padding: 8mm 10mm 7mm; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--amber);
  }
  .masthead .mark { height: 13mm; margin-bottom: 5mm; }
  /* The symbol carries width="500" height="500" attributes. Setting only the
     height leaves a 500px-wide box with the artwork centred inside it, which is
     what set the mark adrift in the middle of the old cover. */
  .masthead .mark svg { display: block; height: 100%; width: auto; }
  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 7.5pt; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .18em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--amber-300); margin: 0 0 2mm;      /* 5.80:1 on charcoal */
  }
  .masthead h1 {
    margin: 0; font-size: 23pt; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -.01em;
  }
  .masthead .sub {
    margin: 2.5mm 0 0; font-size: 9.5pt; line-height: 1.4; max-width: 110mm;
    color: var(--blue-300);                        /* 4.82:1 on charcoal */
  }

  /* ---- provenance rail ------------------------------------------------- */
  /* What was measured, against what, when. Facts in a grid, not a paragraph.
     The old version was a wrapping flex row that landed two items on one line
     and one on each of the next two — a layout that read as a mistake. */
  .rail {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    background: var(--cloud); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  /* Child combinators, not descendants: `.rail div` also matched the .k and .v
     divs nested inside each cell, so every label and every value was drawing
     its own 4mm padding and its own hairline rule. */
  .rail > div { padding: 3.5mm 4mm 4mm; border-left: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .rail > div:first-child { border-left: 0; }
  .rail .k {
    font-size: 7pt; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 1.2mm;     /* 5.11:1 on cloud */
  }
  .rail .v {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-variant-numeric: var(--figures);
    font-size: 7.5pt; line-height: 1.35; color: var(--ink); overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  main { padding: 7mm 0 0; }

  /* ---- headings -------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Charcoal with an amber rule, exactly as .md-typeset h2 is set in the
     documentation. The old blue headings on a blue rule introduced a third
     accent doing a job amber already does across the estate. */
  h2 {
    font-size: 13pt; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -.005em; line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--ink); margin: 8mm 0 3mm;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--amber); padding-bottom: 1.5mm;
    break-after: avoid; break-inside: avoid;
  }
  h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  /* The evidence starts on a fresh page. Without this the table began wherever
     the summary happened to end — two rows stranded at the foot of page one,
     the rest overleaf. Page one is now the whole answer: what was measured,
     what happened, how it was measured. Everything after it is the working.
     The cost is a short report paying for a page break it may not need; the
     benefit is that the first page is always the same shape. */
  h2.evidence { break-before: page; margin-top: 0; }
  /* On screen there is no page to start, so it keeps the normal rhythm; in
     print the margin would push the heading off the top of its fresh page. */
  @media screen { h2.evidence { margin-top: 8mm; } }
  p { margin: 0 0 3mm; orphans: 3; widows: 3; }


  /* ---- the answer ------------------------------------------------------ */
  /* Findings lead, verdict counts follow.

     Counting verdicts is not the finding. On a real comparison the counts read
     "0 faster · 0 slower · 7 unchanged" over a run in which the payload fell
     from 5.7 MB to 1.7 MB, which is both true and the opposite of the story.
     So the cards carry what actually moved, and the tally is demoted to a strip
     underneath that answers "and did anything get slower" in one glance. */
  .findings {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(38mm, 1fr));
    gap: 3mm; margin: 4mm 0 4mm;
  }
  .finding {
    /* min-width: 0 or a long Detail string widens its own grid column and
       shoves every other card out of alignment. */
    min-width: 0; border-top: 1mm solid var(--rule); padding-top: 2.5mm;
    break-inside: avoid;
  }
  .finding .l {
    font-size: 7.5pt; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 1.5mm;          /* 5.58:1 */
  }
  .finding .v {
    font-size: 16pt; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.05;
    font-variant-numeric: var(--figures); overflow-wrap: break-word;
  }
  .finding .d { font-size: 8pt; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 1mm; overflow-wrap: break-word; }
  /* Tone, on the keyline and the number. Not on the label or the detail: those
     are read as text and blue and amber are never body text. */
  .finding.good { border-top-color: var(--success); }
  .finding.good .v { color: var(--success); }         /* 4.94:1 */
  .finding.bad  { border-top-color: var(--error); }
  .finding.bad .v { color: var(--error); }            /* 5.51:1 */
  /* A cost that was accepted on purpose is amber, never red. Tiles that are
     already compressed on the wire have not failed by not shrinking, and a
     deliberate trade is not a regression. */
  .finding.warn { border-top-color: var(--amber); }
  .finding.warn .v { color: var(--amber-900); }       /* 5.52:1 */
  /* No change is neutral. Drawing zero in red would be dishonest in the other
     direction, which is the failure mode nobody checks for. */
  .finding.flat { border-top-color: var(--rule); }
  .finding.flat .v { color: var(--ink); }

  /* One bar, its segments in proportion to the verdict counts.

     The proportions use flex-grow rather than percentages because the template
     has no arithmetic: `flex: 6` beside `flex: 2` is exactly six parts to two,
     divided by the layout engine from the raw counts. */
  .tally { display: flex; width: 100%; height: 7mm; margin: 2mm 0 2mm; overflow: hidden; }
  .tally .seg {
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; min-width: 0;
    color: var(--white); font-size: 10pt; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1;
    font-variant-numeric: var(--figures);
  }
  /* Every fill below carries white type. Success 4.94:1, error 5.51:1, muted
     ink 5.58:1, amber 900 5.52:1, charcoal 11.59:1 — all pass AA at any size,
     which is why brand grey (#8a8b8b, 3.42:1) is not used here. */
  .tally .faster    { background: var(--success); }
  .tally .slower    { background: var(--error); }
  .tally .unchanged { background: var(--muted); }
  .tally .broken    { background: var(--ink); }
  .tally .added     { background: var(--amber-900); }
  .tally .removed   { background: var(--amber); color: var(--ink); }   /* 6.17:1 */

  /* The legend is where the segment colours are named, so that colour is never
     the sole carrier of meaning (WCAG 1.4.1). */
  .legend {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1.5mm 7mm; margin: 0 0 4mm;
    font-size: 9pt; color: var(--ink);
  }
  .legend span { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 2mm; }
  .legend i { width: 3mm; height: 3mm; display: block; align-self: center; }
  .legend .faster i    { background: var(--success); }
  .legend .slower i    { background: var(--error); }
  .legend .unchanged i { background: var(--muted); }
  .legend .broken i    { background: var(--ink); }
  .legend .added i     { background: var(--amber-900); }
  .legend .removed i   { background: var(--amber); }
  .legend b { font-variant-numeric: var(--figures); }

  .lede { font-size: 10.5pt; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--ink); max-width: 145mm; }

  /* ---- callouts -------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Caveats sit above the tally, never below it, so that nobody can reach the
     verdict without first passing the reason to distrust it. */
  .note {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--amber); background: var(--amber-100);
    padding: 2.6mm 4mm; margin: 0 0 3mm; font-size: 9pt;
    max-width: 165mm; break-inside: avoid;
  }
  .note.bad { border-left-color: var(--error); background: var(--error-tint); }
  .note strong {
    display: block; margin-bottom: 1.2mm; color: var(--amber-900);  /* 5.00:1 */
    font-size: 7.5pt; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .note.bad strong { color: var(--error); }      /* 4.90:1 on the error tint */

  /* ---- the table ------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* One table for the whole report, not one per group.

     Each group used to get its own <table>, and `table-layout: auto` sized each
     independently: six tables, six different column grids, no figure landing
     under the figure above it. A single table with `table-layout: fixed` and an
     explicit colgroup gives the document one grid, and — because a fixed layout
     ignores content when sizing — no cell can bully the others.

     The rows are the chart. Each carries a bar, so the shape of the suite is
     legible by scanning the column rather than by reading fourteen numbers. */
  table {
    width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; table-layout: fixed;
    margin: 3mm 0 2mm; font-size: 9pt;
  }
  /* Charcoal header with white type, as the documentation sets its tables.
     display: table-header-group is the default; it is stated because it is the
     thing that reprints these labels at the top of every page. */
  thead { display: table-header-group; }
  th {
    background: var(--ink); color: var(--white);
    text-align: left; font-size: 7.5pt; font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: .07em; text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 2mm;
  }
  th.num { text-align: right; }

  /* One <tbody> per scenario, so break-inside can hold a scenario's figures and
     its caveat together. A caveat printed on the page after the number it
     qualifies is a caveat that does not do its job. */
  tbody.rec { break-inside: avoid; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  td { padding: 2mm; vertical-align: baseline; }

  /* The verdict as a coloured spine down the left edge of the whole record. It
     is the quickest answer to "better or worse" while scanning, and unlike
     coloured text it survives being photocopied. */
  tbody.rec > tr > td:first-child { border-left: 1.2mm solid transparent; }
  tbody.v-faster       > tr > td:first-child { border-left-color: var(--success); }
  tbody.v-slower       > tr > td:first-child { border-left-color: var(--error); }
  tbody.v-unchanged    > tr > td:first-child { border-left-color: var(--rule); }
  tbody.v-broken       > tr > td:first-child { border-left-color: var(--error); }
  tbody.v-added        > tr > td:first-child,
  tbody.v-removed      > tr > td:first-child { border-left-color: var(--amber); }
  /* Styled ahead of the data: the performance work is adding an absolute floor
     below which a difference is timer jitter rather than a result. When those
     scenarios start arriving with Verdict "unmeasurable" they will land here
     looking deliberate rather than unstyled. */
  tbody.v-unmeasurable > tr > td:first-child { border-left-color: var(--muted); }
  /* A broken scenario is not a slow scenario. Tinting the whole record stops it
     being skimmed as one more row of timings. */
  tbody.v-broken > tr > td { background: var(--error-tint); }

  /* The group label rides inside its first scenario's tbody rather than in one
     of its own. That is what makes it structurally impossible for a group
     heading to be orphaned at the foot of a page. */
  tr.group th {
    background: var(--blue-100); color: var(--blue-900);   /* 7.34:1 */
    font-size: 8pt; letter-spacing: .12em; padding: 1.8mm 2mm;
    border-left: 1.2mm solid var(--blue-100);
  }

  .scenario { font-weight: 700; overflow-wrap: break-word; }

  /* Figures. Right-aligned and tabular so the decimal points stack; without
     tabular figures the "0.09 / 27.87 / 4377.34" column wanders a couple of
     millimetres a row and stops being scannable. */
  td.num {
    text-align: right; font-family: var(--mono);
    font-variant-numeric: var(--figures);
    font-feature-settings: "tnum" 1, "lnum" 1, "ss01" 1, "ss02" 1;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  td.bytes { white-space: normal; }   /* "1.7 MB → 1.7 MB" is allowed to wrap */
  /* Percentages never wrap — they have no spaces in them. The multiples do
     ("11.6× faster"), and wrapping is the lesser evil: with nowrap a long label
     overflows leftward and runs into the After column, welding two unrelated
     figures together. */
  td.change { font-weight: 700; white-space: normal; }

  /* Caveats keep their own full-width row, tinted, so they are impossible to
     skim past and do not squeeze the figure columns. Ink on amber 100 is
     10.50:1 — the tint carries the signal, the text stays black. */
  /* The row class and the block class are deliberately different names.
     When both were "caveat", the `.caveat { display: block }` rule matched
     the <tr> too, which stops it being a table row: the cell dropped out of
     the fixed grid and rendered at the width of column one, turning a
     two-line caveat into a column of single words. */
  /* The short caveat is a chip in the row it qualifies — a few words, always
     visible, impossible to skim past. The full explanation is an endnote. Four
     lines of hedging under every affected row used to double the height of the
     table and, by being everywhere, stopped being read anywhere. */
  .caveat {
    display: inline-block; margin-top: 1mm; font-size: 7.5pt; line-height: 1.3;
    background: var(--amber-100); border-left: 2px solid var(--amber);
    padding: .8mm 2mm; color: var(--ink);            /* 10.50:1 on the tint */
  }
  tbody.v-broken .caveat { background: var(--error-tint); border-left-color: var(--error); }

  .endnotes { font-size: 8pt; line-height: 1.4; margin-top: 2mm;
              column-count: 2; column-gap: 8mm; }
  .endnotes div { break-inside: avoid; margin-bottom: 2.2mm; }
  .endnotes dt { font-weight: 700; }
  .endnotes dd { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); }    /* 5.58:1 */

  /* Verdict. A shape as well as a colour, drawn with borders so it needs no
     glyph the font might be missing and no image the palette cannot reach. */
  .verdict {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 1.6mm;
    font-weight: 700; font-size: 8.5pt; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* The base state is the neutral one, and every known verdict overrides it.
     VerdictClass is whatever report.go decides to call the state, so a verdict
     added later — "unmeasurable" is already on its way — arrives looking like a
     deliberate neutral rather than like unstyled text. Styled by name below:
     faster, slower, unchanged, broken, added, removed, unmeasurable. */
  .verdict { color: var(--muted); }                              /* 5.58:1 */
  .verdict::before {
    content: ""; display: block; flex: none;
    width: 2.4mm; height: .9mm; background: var(--muted);
  }
  .verdict.faster { color: var(--success); }                     /* 4.94:1 */
  .verdict.faster::before {
    width: 0; height: 0; background: none;
    border-left: 1.1mm solid transparent; border-right: 1.1mm solid transparent;
    border-top: 1.7mm solid var(--success);
  }
  .verdict.slower, .verdict.broken { color: var(--error); }      /* 5.51:1 */
  .verdict.slower::before {
    width: 0; height: 0; background: none;
    border-left: 1.1mm solid transparent; border-right: 1.1mm solid transparent;
    border-bottom: 1.7mm solid var(--error);
  }
  .verdict.broken::before { width: 2.2mm; height: 2.2mm; background: var(--error); border-radius: 50%; }
  .verdict.unchanged, .verdict.unmeasurable { color: var(--muted); }   /* 5.58:1 */
  .verdict.unchanged::before, .verdict.unmeasurable::before {
    width: 2.4mm; height: .9mm; background: var(--muted);
  }
  .verdict.added, .verdict.removed { color: var(--amber-900); }  /* 5.52:1 */
  .verdict.added::before, .verdict.removed::before {
    width: 1.8mm; height: 1.8mm; background: var(--amber-900); transform: rotate(45deg);
  }

  /* The duration bar.

     It is scaled against the slowest median in the report, so one four-second
     scenario and thirteen sub-millisecond ones produce one full bar and
     thirteen hairlines. That is not a fault in the chart, it is the finding —
     so the column says plainly what it measures, and anything under three
     percent is drawn as a square tick rather than a squashed bar, which reads
     as "negligible against the slowest" instead of "chart is broken".

     The bar is blue, never green or red: it reports duration, not goodness, and
     painting a *faster* verdict's bar green implied the opposite of what the
     length was saying. Colour for the verdict lives on the spine.

     What this scale cannot do is compare two sub-millisecond scenarios with
     each other. That needs a per-group or logarithmic scale computed in
     report.go — see NOTES-design.md for the exact fields. */
  .bar { height: 2.6mm; background: var(--cloud); border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .bar i { display: block; height: 100%; background: var(--blue); }
  .bar i.tick { min-width: 1.4mm; }
  .bar-none { color: var(--muted); font-size: 8.5pt; }

  .scale-note { font-size: 8.5pt; color: var(--muted); max-width: 150mm; margin: 0 0 1mm; }

  /* ---- reference and method ------------------------------------------- */
  /* What each scenario is evidence about used to sit under every row, two or
     three lines of prose per scenario, which tripled the height of the table
     and buried the figures it was supposed to explain. It is the same text,
     moved to an appendix and set two-up at reference size. */
  .glossary { column-count: 2; column-gap: 8mm; font-size: 8pt; line-height: 1.4; margin-top: 2mm; }
  .glossary div { break-inside: avoid; margin-bottom: 2.2mm; }
  .glossary dt { font-weight: 700; }
  .glossary dd { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); }      /* 5.58:1 */

  /* Four short lines from report.go, set two-up, in place of the two
     paragraphs this template used to hard-code — which could and did
     contradict the sample count and the noise floor actually used. */
  .method { margin: 0 0 3mm; padding: 0; list-style: none; font-size: 9pt;
            max-width: 160mm; break-inside: avoid;
            column-count: 2; column-gap: 8mm; }
  .method li { break-inside: avoid; margin-bottom: 1.5mm; padding-left: 4mm; position: relative; }
  /* A drawn marker rather than a bullet character: list-style glyphs come from
     the font, and this document cannot assume any font but its own. */
  .method li::before {
    content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 1.5mm;
    width: 1.6mm; height: 1.6mm; background: var(--blue);
  }
  .fineprint { font-size: 8.5pt; color: var(--muted); max-width: 150mm; margin: 0;
               break-before: avoid; break-inside: avoid; }

  /* ---- footer ---------------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Blue 900, the colour the documentation's footer already uses. */
  footer {
    margin: 9mm -16mm 0; padding: 5mm 16mm; background: var(--blue-900);
    color: var(--blue-100); font-size: 8.5pt; break-inside: avoid;
  }
  footer p { margin: 0; }
  footer a { color: var(--amber-300); text-decoration: none;   /* 5.80:1 */
             border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(245,209,143,.45); }
  /* The credit line is specified with a heart emoji. It is drawn here instead,
     because on a machine without an emoji font — which is every build container
     this report is produced on — the emoji prints as a hollow tofu box, and a
     missing glyph in the one line carrying the Kartoza name is worse than a
     substituted shape. Amber 500, on brand, and it always renders. */
  .heart { display: inline-block; vertical-align: -.15em; width: 1em; height: 1em; }

  .mono { font-family: var(--mono); }

  /* ---- print ----------------------------------------------------------- */
  @media print {
    body { background: var(--white); font-size: 9.5pt; }
    .sheet { max-width: none; padding: 0; border: 0; }
    /* The band already sits on the page margin, so it needs a modest inset of
       its own rather than the 16mm the screen sheet gives it. */
    footer { margin-left: 0; margin-right: 0; padding: 5mm 8mm; }
  }

  @media screen {
    tbody.rec:hover > tr > td { background: var(--blue-100); }
    tbody.v-broken:hover > tr > td { background: var(--error-tint); }
  }

  /* Narrow screens: the rail is the only thing that cannot survive four
     columns, and print margins are wasteful on a phone. */
  @media screen and (max-width: 640px) {
    .sheet { padding: 8mm 5mm 0; }
    /* Seven columns of figures do not fit a phone, and a fixed layout will not
       reflow them into something legible. The table scrolls inside its own box
       rather than dragging the whole page sideways with it. Screen only: an
       overflow container on a table stops Chromium paginating it, which would
       cost the report its page breaks. */
    .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
    .table-scroll table { min-width: 165mm; }
    .rail { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
    .rail > div:nth-child(3) { border-left: 0; }
    .glossary { column-count: 1; }
    .method { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0 0; }
    .method dd { margin-bottom: 2mm; }
    footer { margin-left: -5mm; margin-right: -5mm; padding-left: 5mm; padding-right: 5mm; }
  }
</style>
<style id="brand-typeface">
/* The report's two typefaces, embedded as data URIs.

   They have to be embedded. The report is one self-contained file by design,
   and the machines that generate and read it — build containers, CI runners —
   carry almost no fonts: on this project's container fontconfig knows exactly
   one family. Asked for a face it does not have, headless Chromium logged
   `TextRunHarfBuzz error … glyph_count: 0` and printed a PDF with every rule,
   bar and block of colour intact and not one glyph on it. Whatever else is
   wrong with a report, it must not be invisible.

   Which faces, and why not Nunito. The palette names Nunito and JetBrains
   Mono. JetBrains Mono is here. Nunito is not, because nixpkgs ships it only
   as OpenType and this dev shell has no subsetting tool: three static weights
   would cost about a megabyte of base64 and still leave no italic. Source
   Sans 3 stands in — already vendored in this repository under
   frontend/src/assets/fonts, refreshed from nixpkgs by `dt vendor-fonts`,
   OFL-1.1 like Nunito, and variable, so 166 KB spans weights 200–900 and the
   headings get a drawn extra-bold rather than a synthesised one. It is also
   the face the Decision Theatre application sets its own headings in, so the
   report matches the product it measures. Vendoring a Nunito woff2 and
   swapping the payload below is the path to full fidelity; NOTES-design.md
   says what that needs.

   Both are variable fonts, which is what makes the cost bearable: two files
   cover every weight this design uses, where static cuts would need six.

   The base64 is pasted here because report.go — which would otherwise
   go:embed the two woff2 files sitting beside this template and pass them in —
   belongs to someone else this week. NOTES-design.md names the field to add so
   these two blobs can be deleted. The files are in the package regardless, so
   the blobs have a checkable source rather than being loose binary in a
   template. */
@font-face {
  font-family: "Report Sans";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 200 900;
  /* swap, not block: if the payload below ever failed to decode, block
     would hide the text for three seconds before falling through, and a
     print snapshot taken inside that window is a blank page. */
  font-display: swap;
  /* internal/bench/report-sans.woff2, byte-identical to frontend/src/assets/fonts/SourceSans3VF-Upright.woff2 — 169528 bytes, sha256 5e52ee9a987f1c626dec8a034a8498176591c55cfe66389891665d051e8423d6. SIL Open Font License 1.1, Copyright 2010-2023 Adobe. */
  src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2");
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "Report Mono";
  font-style: normal;
  font-weight: 100 800;
  /* swap, not block: if the payload below ever failed to decode, block
     would hide the text for three seconds before falling through, and a
     print snapshot taken inside that window is a blank page. */
  font-display: swap;
  /* internal/bench/report-mono.woff2, from nixpkgs jetbrains-mono, share/fonts/WOFF2/JetBrainsMono[wght].woff2 — 111744 bytes, sha256 e3d5e9245dda34ab9253ef47a96bb5d1ebfc8ae2b598767b5f2ea12bdb109131. SIL Open Font License 1.1, Copyright 2020 JetBrains s.r.o. */
  src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2");
}

  /* What landed between the builds. Last in the document on purpose: it narrows
     the search for a cause, and a reader who meets it first may read a
     correlation as one. */
  .changes { margin-top: 8mm; }
  .changes-intro { max-width: 62ch; }
  .changes-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; table-layout: fixed; margin-top: 3mm; }
  .changes-table th, .changes-table td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top;
    padding: 1.6mm 2mm 1.6mm 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .changes-table th { font-size: 8pt; letter-spacing: .05em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; }
  .changes-table td.num { width: 9ch; }
  .changes-table td:nth-child(2) { width: 13ch; }
  .changes-table .branch { display: block; font-size: 8.5pt; color: var(--muted); }
  .changes-table .none { color: var(--muted); }
  .changes-table a { color: var(--blue-900); }
  @media print {
    .changes { break-before: page; }
    .changes-table tbody tr { break-inside: avoid; }
    .changes-table thead { display: table-header-group; }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="sheet">

  <header class="masthead">
    {{ with .Mark }}<div class="mark">{{ . }}</div>{{ end }}
    <p class="eyebrow">Decision Theatre · dtbench</p>
    <h1>{{ .Title }}</h1>
    {{ with .Subtitle }}<p class="sub">{{ . }}</p>{{ end }}
  </header>

  <section class="rail">
    <div><div class="k">Baseline</div><div class="v">{{ .BaselineLabel }}</div></div>
    <div><div class="k">Compared with</div><div class="v">{{ .CurrentLabel }}</div></div>
    <div><div class="k">Target</div><div class="v">{{ .TargetLabel }}</div></div>
    <div><div class="k">Generated</div><div class="v">{{ .GeneratedAt }}</div></div>
  </section>

  <main>

    <h2>The short answer</h2>

    {{/* Anything that undermines the comparison is printed before the
         comparison, so the tally cannot be read in isolation. */}}
    {{ if .Broken }}
    <div class="note bad">
      <strong>{{ len .Broken }} scenario(s) stopped working</strong>
      A scenario that fails is usually <em>faster</em>, so these are excluded from the timings rather
      than counted as improvements: {{ .BrokenNames }}.
    </div>
    {{ end }}

    {{ if .NotRun }}
    <div class="note">
      <strong>{{ .NotRun }} scenario(s) did not run</strong>
      They are absent from every count below rather than folded into &ldquo;unchanged&rdquo;: a scenario
      that did not run is not evidence that nothing changed. {{ .NotRunNames }}.
    </div>
    {{ end }}

    {{ range .Warnings }}
    <div class="note"><strong>Read with care</strong>{{ . }}</div>
    {{ end }}

    {{/* The findings come before the tally, and the tally before the lede: what
         moved, then whether anything got slower, then the sentence. */}}
    <div class="findings">
      {{ range .Findings }}
      <div class="finding {{ .Tone }}">
        <div class="l">{{ .Label }}</div>
        <div class="v">{{ .Value }}</div>
        {{ with .Detail }}<div class="d">{{ . }}</div>{{ end }}
      </div>
      {{ end }}
    </div>

    <div class="tally">
      <span class="seg faster"    style="flex:{{ .Headline.Faster }}">{{ if .Headline.Faster }}{{ .Headline.Faster }}{{ end }}</span>
      <span class="seg slower"    style="flex:{{ .Headline.Slower }}">{{ if .Headline.Slower }}{{ .Headline.Slower }}{{ end }}</span>
      <span class="seg unchanged" style="flex:{{ .Headline.Unchanged }}">{{ if .Headline.Unchanged }}{{ .Headline.Unchanged }}{{ end }}</span>
      <span class="seg broken"    style="flex:{{ .Headline.Broken }}">{{ if .Headline.Broken }}{{ .Headline.Broken }}{{ end }}</span>
      <span class="seg added"     style="flex:{{ .Headline.Added }}">{{ if .Headline.Added }}{{ .Headline.Added }}{{ end }}</span>
      <span class="seg removed"   style="flex:{{ .Headline.Removed }}">{{ if .Headline.Removed }}{{ .Headline.Removed }}{{ end }}</span>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <span class="faster"><i></i><b>{{ .Headline.Faster }}</b> faster</span>
      <span class="slower"><i></i><b>{{ .Headline.Slower }}</b> slower</span>
      <span class="unchanged"><i></i><b>{{ .Headline.Unchanged }}</b> unchanged</span>
      {{ if .Headline.Broken }}<span class="broken"><i></i><b>{{ .Headline.Broken }}</b> broken</span>{{ end }}
      {{ if .Headline.Added }}<span class="added"><i></i><b>{{ .Headline.Added }}</b> new</span>{{ end }}
      {{ if .Headline.Removed }}<span class="removed"><i></i><b>{{ .Headline.Removed }}</b> gone</span>{{ end }}
    </div>

    <p class="lede">{{ .Lede }}</p>

    <h2 class="evidence">Scenario by scenario</h2>
    <p class="scale-note">
      Before and after are median times. The bar is that scenario&rsquo;s median against the
      slowest scenario in this report — a linear scale, so most of the suite shows as a tick.
      Against the slowest request, it is one.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      {{/* Fixed widths, declared once for the document, so every group's
           figures land in the same place on the page. */}}
      <colgroup>
        <col style="width:23%">
        <col style="width:11%">
        <col style="width:11%">
        <col style="width:14%">
        <col style="width:14%">
        <col style="width:13%">
        <col style="width:14%">
      </colgroup>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Before</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">After</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Change</th>
          <th scope="col">vs. slowest</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Bytes</th>
          <th scope="col">Verdict</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>

      {{ range .Groups }}
      {{ $group := .Name }}
      {{ range $i, $d := .Deltas }}
      <tbody class="rec v-{{ $d.VerdictClass }}">
        {{ if eq $i 0 }}
        <tr class="group"><th scope="colgroup" colspan="7">{{ $group }}</th></tr>
        {{ end }}
        <tr>
          <td>
            <span class="scenario">{{ $d.Name }}</span>
            {{ with $d.Caveat }}<span class="caveat">{{ . }}</span>{{ end }}
          </td>
          <td class="num">{{ $d.BeforeMs }}</td>
          <td class="num">{{ $d.AfterMs }}</td>
          <td class="num change">{{ $d.ChangeLabel }}</td>
          <td>
            {{ if $d.BarWidth }}
            <div class="bar"><i class="{{ if lt $d.BarWidth 3 }}tick{{ end }}" style="width:{{ $d.BarWidth }}%"></i></div>
            {{ else }}
            <span class="bar-none">—</span>
            {{ end }}
          </td>
          <td class="num bytes">{{ $d.BytesLabel }}</td>
          <td><span class="verdict {{ $d.VerdictClass }}">{{ $d.VerdictLabel }}</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
      {{ end }}
      {{ end }}
    </table>
    </div>

    {{/* Only rendered when something needs hedging, so a clean comparison does
         not carry an empty heading. */}}
    {{ $anyLong := false }}{{ range .Groups }}{{ range .Deltas }}{{ if .CaveatLong }}{{ $anyLong = true }}{{ end }}{{ end }}{{ end }}
    {{ if $anyLong }}
    <h2>Caveats in full</h2>
    <dl class="endnotes">
      {{ range .Groups }}{{ range .Deltas }}{{ $name := .Name }}{{ with .CaveatLong }}
      <div><dt>{{ $name }}</dt><dd>{{ . }}</dd></div>
      {{ end }}{{ end }}{{ end }}
    </dl>
    {{ end }}

    <h2>What each scenario measures</h2>
    {{/* Group headings are left out here: the scenario names carry the grouping
         well enough at reference size, and a heading inside a two-column flow
         lands wherever the column break puts it. */}}
    <dl class="glossary">
      {{ range .Groups }}
      {{ range .Deltas }}
      {{ $name := .Name }}
      {{ with .Why }}
      <div><dt>{{ $name }}</dt><dd>{{ . }}</dd></div>
      {{ end }}
      {{ end }}
      {{ end }}
    </dl>

    <h2>How this was measured</h2>
    {{/* The lines are report.go's, not the template's: they carry the actual
         sample count and the actual noise floor, which a hard-coded sentence
         here could and did contradict. */}}
    <ul class="method">
      {{ range .Method }}<li>{{ . }}</li>{{ end }}
    </ul>
    <p class="fineprint">
      These runs come from a working machine at sample counts in the tens. A tighter threshold would
      produce confident verdicts that a rerun contradicts, so where a verdict rests on distributions
      that overlap, the row says so.
    </p>

    {{ if or .Changes.PRs .Changes.Unavailable }}
    <section class="changes">
      <h2>What landed between these builds</h2>
      {{ if .Changes.PRs }}
      <p class="changes-intro">
        {{ len .Changes.PRs }} pull request{{ if ne (len .Changes.PRs) 1 }}s{{ end }}
        {{ if .Changes.Commits }}and {{ .Changes.Commits }} commit{{ if ne .Changes.Commits 1 }}s{{ end }}{{ end }}
        merged in <span class="mono">{{ .Changes.From }}..{{ .Changes.To }}</span>.
        This narrows the search for a cause; it does not establish one. Several of these can move the
        same scenario, in opposite directions.
      </p>
      <table class="changes-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">PR</th><th scope="col">Merged</th><th scope="col">Change</th><th scope="col">Issues</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {{ range .Changes.PRs }}
          <tr>
            <td class="mono num"><a href="{{ $.RepoURL }}/pull/{{ .Number }}">#{{ .Number }}</a></td>
            <td class="mono">{{ .Date }}</td>
            <td>{{ .Title }}<span class="branch mono">{{ .Branch }}</span></td>
            <td class="mono num">
              {{ if .Issues }}{{ range $i, $n := .Issues }}{{ if $i }} {{ end -}}
                <a href="{{ $.RepoURL }}/issues/{{ $n }}">#{{ $n }}</a>
              {{- end }}{{ else }}<span class="none">&mdash;</span>{{ end }}
            </td>
          </tr>
          {{ end }}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      {{ if .Changes.Issues }}
      <p class="fineprint">
        Issues referenced across the range:
        {{ range $i, $n := .Changes.Issues }}{{ if $i }}, {{ end -}}
          <a href="{{ $.RepoURL }}/issues/{{ $n }}">#{{ $n }}</a>
        {{- end }}.
      </p>
      {{ end }}
      {{ else }}
      <p class="fineprint">{{ .Changes.Unavailable }}</p>
      {{ end }}
    </section>
    {{ end }}

  </main>

  <footer>
    <p>
      Generated by <span class="mono">dtbench</span> · Decision Theatre.
      Made with
      <svg class="heart" viewBox="0 0 16 16" role="img" aria-label="love" focusable="false">
        <path d="M8 14.2 2.6 8.9a3.4 3.4 0 0 1 4.8-4.8L8 4.7l.6-.6a3.4 3.4 0 0 1 4.8 4.8z" fill="{{ .Brand.Amber500 }}"/>
      </svg>
      by <a href="https://kartoza.com">Kartoza</a> ·
      <a href="{{ .SponsorURL }}">Donate!</a> ·
      <a href="{{ .RepoURL }}">GitHub</a>
    </p>
  </footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
